 but  there's also sometimes a more practical  matter you may have noticed that nearly  all the examples i've cited so far have  been from movies and barely anything  from tv  and that's because redeeming a character  by means other than death isn't just  more work it takes more  time and to be blunt about it  movies don't usually have the time to do  this  these kinds of heel face turns usually  happen in or around the climax so like  the last 20 minutes of a two-hour movie  and after that there's not going to be  enough time to assure the audience of  the legitimacy of that redemption  so  death is the obvious solution since it  means it's basically a closed question  even if audience is a little bit divided  on whether they buy that this is fully  redeeming or not it's at this point just  an academic debate because it doesn't  impact the actual story while  everything's getting wrapped up because  the character in question isn't around  anymore that's not to say that there  aren't redemption stories in movies that  do take their time but the thing is  those are generally redemptions for  morally compromised heroes  not villains so you can take something  like the unforgiven or in bruges these  are definitely redemption stories but  the things the characters are trying to  redeem themselves for are a background  feature it's something that happened in  the past and if it's shown at all it's  in a flashback so that the focus can be  on the redemption for the bulk of the  movie again taking the time needed  but that's why this is a treatment  generally afforded to heroes or  anti-heroes  you have to go to long-form formats like  tv and that is where you get stories  that put in the work to redeem actual  villains through means other than death  we're talking about characters like  prince zuko from avatar the last  airbender who undergoes a super slow  redemption over the course of the latter  two seasons of the show a big chunk of  stranger things seasons two is spent  reinventing steve harrington the generic  high school jerk from the first season  the third season of infinity train is  largely a slow process of  self-realization and then redemption for  the character of grace jamie lannister  is a hell of a redemption arc in game of  thrones  up until that last season anyway but  like that thing did everybody dirty but  my own personal favorite here is catra  from shira and the princesses of power  catra was a wonderfully complex villain  working for the big bad but driven by  her own personal agenda and  massive amounts of baggage and trauma  now in the third season she went so far  as to try and end the god damn world  just despite her best frenemy and future  love interest adora now by the end of  season four it was clear that catra was  reeling from everything that had gone on  up to that point but she couldn't seem  to break from her most destructive  behavior  it's you  you drive them away wildcat and prior to  the release of season five the last  season of the show  i was  incredibly nervous i actually made a  whole video at the time expressing my  suspicion that the show was going to try  and redeem catra and ultimately pair her  off with adora and  i had a lot of apprehension about that  idea because since i like  most people watching it suspected the  two were going to end up together at the  end i figured redemption through death  was off the table which  i mean it's  good because i don't like that anyway  but i just couldn't see how the show was  going to be able to have that kind of  ending for a character  this dangerous and this toxic who'd done  this much damage to virtually everybody  around her  well the show writers are clearly  smarter than me because they did make it  work  three episodes into the fifth season  catra tries to actually do the heroic  sacrifice  i want to do that one good thing in my  life she betrays horde prime to help  glimmer escape his ship and she's  clearly shocked when two episodes later  adoring companies show up to rescue her  catra tried to take the easy way out do  one good thing  and then be gone  but neither the show nor the characters  we're going to let it go down like that  and her entire arc for the next eight  episodes  is about her working actively on getting  better and it's  really well done because it includes  brief relapses in behavior and catra's  growing self-awareness of her own issues  and open acknowledgement that getting  better about them is going to take time  and be a long-term process i'm sorry i  got angry it's something i'm working on  oh  you are yes now can you please  yes i am it doesn't cheap out and  pretend that the attempted sacrifice  thing means oh she's good now no she's  got a lot of work to do and has to want  to do it and the show  gets  that  gets it with catra anyway unfortunately  given that i'm talking about redemption  in general i would kind of be remiss not  to acknowledge that the show drops the  ball with hordak who doesn't really look  like he's gonna face justice for  anything he does  so are we all just like okay with this  quit side tangent about that whole thing  the fact that hordak getting let off the  hook ended up rubbing so many people the  wrong way kind of illustrates what a  fine line return of the jedi was able to  walk because part of the reason that one  works is that it's focused on redeeming  vader in the eyes of luke and luke alone  luke is the reason he turns luke is the  only one who witnesses it luke was  really the only one invested in vader's  redemption at all  so showing vader redeemed in his son's  eyes is what was needed  but the hordak thing shows  what would happen  if vader had lived because shira tried  to redeem hordak through entrapta and  also have him live  but literally nobody else has a reason  to want redemption for hordak plus  entrapta doesn't have the same moral  compass as the rest of the group anyway  so it's just  weird  like in a bad way  [Music]  now i mentioned earlier that i had  another reason for not liking redemption  equals death other than just feeling  that it's the cheap and easy way out and  this one is going to get a bit more  philosophical and dig into more  moral societal standards and  expectations  before i get into this  i don't think that anything i'm about to  talk about is the intended message of  redemption through death stories  i always say  that you don't have to do it on purpose  because the pattern is the problem and  the patterns and stories and the things  that repeat can cause some troubling  framing for how we view and think about  things  so my gut level problem with redemption  through death  is that it creates a scenario where  someone who has done  awful things  can be redeemed through  one good act of sufficient size  in narrative it's a single act because  well they're dead now so they can't do  anything else after that anyway but  extrapolating from that to real world  application i have major issues with  this very concept because you know most  people aren't going to be part of a plan  that will open a hell portal to a demon  dimension but we all do things that hurt  other people at some points in our lives  and the perpetuation of redemption  through death  paints a world where your sins can be  forgiven  by a counter balancing act  it makes  morals a transactional thing  where a bad thing can be paid for by a  good thing  coming back to earlier where i said a  common reason for this trope is because  the bad things that the characters did  were so bad they can't be repaid any  other way and don't get me wrong i'm not  saying you shouldn't try to correct  damage or that people aren't deserving  of a second chance or whatever but you  have to go into doing that knowing that  you are not owed forgiveness and you  have to do it because it's the right  thing to do not because you think it'll  wipe your slate clean  my big problem with this transactional  moral framework  is that the people it empowers are the  people who are doing the damage  it creates a situation where somebody  who's hurt you  says sorry or brings flowers or whatever  and is expected to just think  everything's fine  the death's been paid it's transactional  and it also pressures the person who was  hurt or wronged to forgive them  how many times have you found yourself  in a situation where you feel pressure  to forgive someone who you just can't  even if it's just not right now  maybe you're feeling direct pressure  from that person or other people around  you or maybe just the general societal  pressure because well  they did a nice thing to make up for it  so  it's supposed to be okay now  this is essentially the same core  problem that i have with the whole idea  of well  you have to forgive family because their  family because it puts the burden on the  injured  to forgive  and perpetuates the idea that  forgiveness is owed and it's  not it's earned and only the people who  were hurt can decide whether redemption  has been achieved or is even possible it  is not  up to the person who did the damage and  it's definitely not up to an outside  observer  and it gets worse because if morality is  treated as being transactional that  doesn't actually encourage people to be  good  instead it says that it's perfectly fine  to be bad as long as you balance it out  later it says that it's okay  to break things as long as you donate  enough glue later on  i'm gonna circle back  to shira one more time  talk about the death  of shadow weaver  if you haven't seen the show she's a bit  of a wild card villain one who has her  own agenda and goals she started out in  the employ of hordak but split when it  didn't suit her purposes anymore and  she's just  the absolute worst she's a monster who  continually emotionally abused or  manipulated adora and catra throughout  their entire lives and in the final  episode she goes to save catra the  person she's abused more than any other  and of course  dies in the process on first viewing  this annoyed the hell out of me because  i thought the show tried to redeem one  of the most deplorable villains through  a heroic sacrifice but  on rewatch that's not actually what's  going on  yes catcher tells her not to do it yes  both she and adora cry but i mean it's  their mother figure so awful as she was  this is going to stir up emotions but  the framing  here is  really  important because shadow weaver clearly  intends this to be her redemption  she makes a big show of it even takes  her mask off for the first time in the  entire show in her mind this is her big  hero moment where all is forgiven  hell she actually says  [ __ ] nobody said thank you but what  makes it clear to me that the makers of  the show understood that she was not  actually redeemed was that after this  when adora and catcher move forward the  camera pans back down to shadow weaver's  mask firstly visually the show is  reminding us of the face of the monster  not the face she showed us at the very  end  once in order to gain sympathy and to  cap it all off the music in that moment  isn't triumphant it isn't even mournful  it's this  yeah  they understood that she was not  redeemed by that act even though she  clearly thought she was  so more than anything for me  the thing that really rubs me the wrong  way about redemption through death is  the subtextual message about morality  that the trope sends  the message that it's possible to be  redeemed by a single big act instead of  putting in the long-term work to  actively be better regardless of whether  or not the people you wronged actually  forgive you  real life redemption  has to be about being better in and of  itself not balancing out some kind of  moral ledger  as i said i suspect that these are  unintended messages in nearly all cases  but they're there nonetheless  [Music]  while it is possible to make redemption  through death work more often than not  i find it to 